<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-2-parametric-length" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Arc Length and Area of Parametric Curves</title>

  <p><alert>Arclength.</alert> After applying derivatives to parametric curves, we now apply integrals,
  which compute the size or bulk of geometric objects. The most natural measure of the size of a curve is its arclength. We already computed this for graph curves <m>y=f(x)</m> in <m>\S8.1</m>,
  and now we do the more general parametric case.</p>

  <p>We follow the general scheme for computing any measure of size of a geometric object
  from <m>\S5.2</m>.
  We want the arclength <m>L</m> of a parametric curve <m>(x(t),y(t))</m> for <m>t\in[a,b]</m>.
  We cut the curve into <m>n</m> bits determined by <m>\Delta t</m>-increments of <m>t\in[a,b]</m>.</p>

  <figure xml:id="fig-10-2-length-placeholder">
    <caption>Placeholder for 10-2-Length.</caption>
    <image width="72%">
      <shortdescription>Boilerplate TikZ placeholder for 10-2-Length.png.</shortdescription>
      <latex-image>
        <![CDATA[
        \begin{tikzpicture}
          \begin{scope}[x=1.25cm,y=1.0cm]
            \def\ta{0.45}
            \def\tb{4.35}

            \draw[->] (0,0) -- (5.3,0) node[right] {$x$};
            \draw[->] (0,0) -- (0,3.35) node[above] {$y$};

            \draw[thick,domain=0.3:4.7,samples=220,smooth]
              plot (\x,{0.55+0.42*\x+0.22*sin((2.1*\x) r)});
            \node[above right] at (3.05,2.45) {$(x(t),y(t))$};

            \foreach \k in {0,1,2,3,4,5,6} {
              \pgfmathsetmacro\tt{0.45+0.55*\k}
              \pgfmathsetmacro\xx{\tt}
              \pgfmathsetmacro\yy{0.55+0.42*\tt+0.22*sin((2.1*\tt) r)}
              \fill (\xx,\yy) circle (1.0pt);
              \draw[densely dotted] (\xx,0) -- (\xx,\yy);
            }

            \draw[orange!80!black,thick]
              (0.45,{0.55+0.42*0.45+0.22*sin((2.1*0.45) r)}) --
              (1.00,{0.55+0.42*1.00+0.22*sin((2.1*1.00) r)}) --
              (1.55,{0.55+0.42*1.55+0.22*sin((2.1*1.55) r)}) --
              (2.10,{0.55+0.42*2.10+0.22*sin((2.1*2.10) r)}) --
              (2.65,{0.55+0.42*2.65+0.22*sin((2.1*2.65) r)}) --
              (3.20,{0.55+0.42*3.20+0.22*sin((2.1*3.20) r)}) --
              (3.75,{0.55+0.42*3.75+0.22*sin((2.1*3.75) r)});

            \pgfmathsetmacro\ti{2.10}
            \pgfmathsetmacro\tj{2.65}
            \pgfmathsetmacro\xi{\ti}
            \pgfmathsetmacro\xj{\tj}
            \pgfmathsetmacro\yi{0.55+0.42*\ti+0.22*sin((2.1*\ti) r)}
            \pgfmathsetmacro\yj{0.55+0.42*\tj+0.22*sin((2.1*\tj) r)}

            \draw[blue!70!black,thick] (\xi,\yi) -- (\xj,\yj);
            \draw[dashed] (\xi,\yi) -- (\xj,\yi);
            \draw[dashed] (\xj,\yi) -- (\xj,\yj);

            \node[below] at (0.45,0) {$t_0=a$};
            \node[below] at (\xi,0) {$t_i$};
            \node[below] at (\xj,0) {$t_{i+1}$};
            \node[below] at (3.75,0) {$t_n=b$};

            \draw[<->] (\xi,-0.24) -- (\xj,-0.24);
            \node[below] at ({0.5*(\xi+\xj)},-0.24) {$\Delta t$};

            \draw[<->] (\xj+0.12,\yi) -- (\xj+0.12,\yj);
            \node[right] at (\xj+0.12,{0.5*(\yi+\yj)}) {$\Delta y$};

            \draw[<->] (\xi,\yi-0.12) -- (\xj,\yi-0.12);
            \node[below] at ({0.5*(\xi+\xj)},\yi-0.12) {$\Delta x$};

            \node[above left] at ({0.5*(\xi+\xj)},{0.5*(\yi+\yj)}) {$\Delta L_i$};
          \end{scope}
        \end{tikzpicture}
        ]]>
      </latex-image>
    </image>
  </figure>

  <p>Because the bit at the sample point <m>t_i</m> is so short,
  it is well approximated by a straight segment, and we can
  use the Pythagorean Theorem to compute its length:</p>

  <p><md><![CDATA[
\Delta L_i  \ \approx\ \sqrt{(\Delta x)^2+(\Delta y)^2}
\ =\
\sqrt{\frac{(\Delta x)^2+(\Delta y)^2}{(\Delta t)^2}}\  \Delta t
\ =\
\sqrt{(\tfrac{\Delta x}{\Delta t})^2+(\tfrac{\Delta y}{\Delta t})^2}\  \Delta t\,.
  ]]></md></p>

  <p>In the limit as <m>n\to \infty</m>, we get <m>\Delta t\to0</m> and <m>\frac{\Delta x}{\Delta t}\to \frac{dx}{dt} = x'(t_i)</m>; similarly for <m>\frac{\Delta y}{\Delta t}</m>:</p>

  <p><md><![CDATA[
L \ =\ \lim_{n\to\infty} \sum_{i=1}^n\Delta L_i \ =\ \lim_{n\to\infty}\sum_{i=1}^n\! \sqrt{(\tfrac{\Delta x}{\Delta t})^2+(\tfrac{\Delta y}{\Delta t})^2}\ \Delta t
\ = \ \int_a^b\!\!\! \sqrt{(\tfrac{dx}{dt})^2+(\tfrac{dy}{dt})^2}\ dt\,.
  ]]></md></p>

  <p>In Newton notation:</p>

  <p><md><![CDATA[
L  \ = \ \int_a^b\!\!\! \sqrt{x'(t)^2+y'(t)^2}\ dt\,.
  ]]></md></p>

  <p>In fact, the integrand is just the total speed of the particle at time <m>t</m>, combining the horizontal and vertical speeds. To be precise, <m>L</m> is the total distance traveled along the curve;
  if the trajectory repeats its motion around a closed loop, or if it changes direction and backtracks,
  this is larger than the geometric length of the curve.</p>

  <example xml:id="ex-10-2-circle-circumference">
    <statement>
      <p>Compute the circumference length of a circle of radius <m>r</m> using the
        standard parametrization <md>(x(t),y(t))=(r \cos(t), r\sin(t))</md> for <m>t\in[0,2\pi]</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
      We start by computing the derivative, which is 

      <md>(x'(t),y'(t)) = (-r\sin(t),r\cos(t)).</md> 
      Therefore, its length is:</p>

      <p><md>
        <mrow> L \amp = \int_0^{2\pi} \sqrt{(-r\sin(t))^2 + (r\cos(t))^2}\,dt </mrow>
        <mrow> \amp = \int_0^{2\pi} r \, \sqrt{\sin^2(t) + \cos^2(t)}\,dt</mrow>
        <mrow> \amp =\ \int_0^{2\pi} r \,dt = \left.\vphantom{\sum}rt\,\right|_{t=0}^{t=2\pi} \ =\ 2\pi r.</mrow>
      </md></p>

      <p>
        The integral is so easy because the particle travels at constant speed <m>r</m>.
        This was much harder in <m>\S8.1</m>, using our previous formula <m>L = \int_{-r}^r\! \sqrt{1{+}f'(x)^2}\ dx</m>, where <m>f(x)=\sqrt{r^2-x^2}</m>.
      </p>
      </solution>
  </example>

  <example xml:id="ex-10-2-cycloid-length">
    <statement>
      <p>Find the length of one arch of the cycloid from <m>\S10.1</m>.</p>
    </statement>

    <solution>
      <p>
        As in 10.1, a parameterization of the cycloid is
      <md>(x(t),y(t))=(t{-}\sin(t), 1{-}\cos(t)).</md>
      
      To get just the first arch, we only consider <m>t\in[0,2\pi]</m>. We then compute the derivative which is
       <md>(x'(t),y'(t))= (1{-}\cos(t),\, \sin(t)).</md> 
      
      Integrating this quantity gives the length of the arch:</p>

      <p><md>
        <mrow> L \amp = \int_0^{2\pi}\!\!\! \sqrt{(1{-}\cos(t))^2+(\sin(t))^2}\,dt </mrow>
        <mrow> \amp = \int_0^{2\pi} \sqrt{1{-}2\cos(t)+\cos^2(t)+\sin^2(t)}\,dt</mrow>
        <mrow> \amp = \int_0^{2\pi} \sqrt{2(1{-}\cos(t))}\,dt</mrow>
        <mrow>\amp = \int_0^{2\pi} 2\sin(\tfrac t2)\,dt\ = 8.</mrow>
      </md></p>

      <p>Here we used the identity <m>\sin(\frac t2) =\sqrt{\frac{1-\cos(t)}2}</m>.</p>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <subsection xml:id="ssec-parametric-area">
    <title> Area</title>

    <p> 
      Consider a parametric curve <m>(x(t),y(t))</m> for <m>t\in [a,b]</m>
      which closes into a loop starting and ending at the point <m>(x(a),y(a))=(x(b),y(b))</m>.
      Then the enclosed area is:
    </p>

    <p><md>
      A  = \pm \int_a^b y(t) x'(t)\,dt \ =\ \mp \int_a^b x(t) y'(t)\,dt,
    </md></p>

    <p>where the signs after the two equalities are <m>+,-</m> if the loop travels clockwise around the region,
    but <m>-,+</m> if it travels counterclockwise.</p>

    <p>As an exercise, prove these formulas by slice analysis.
    The point is that area is added while traveling in one direction,
    then subtracted while traveling back.</p>
    
  </subsection>

</section>